Derived from the following Decepticon source:

  tools/bash/bash.py + tools/bash/prompt.py
      The interactive persistent-session engine: NAMED tmux sessions with
      per-session cwd/env/background-jobs, the tmux drive with a PS1/PROMPT_COMMAND
      marker for completion + prompt detection, pipe-pane session logging,
      AUTOMATIC INTERACTIVE-PROMPT DETECTION (msfconsole / sliver / evil-winrm /
      REPLs), the background + auto-background-at-60s lifecycle with a notify-once
      completion, output tiering (inline / scratch / watchdog), ANSI stripping +
      repetitive-line compression, and the wedged-session + tmux pipe-degradation
      recovery ladders (backend/cockpit/session_engine.py).

  tools/opplan.py

  tools/opplan.py
      The OPPLAN data model — Objective, OPPLAN, ObjectivePhase, ObjectiveStatus,
      C2Tier, OpsecLevel — and the objective status STATE MACHINE
      (pending -> in-progress -> completed / blocked / cancelled, with completed
      and cancelled terminal), plus the versioned-payload builder and summary.

  tools/defense/conops.py
      The Concept of Operations document structure and generator shape.

  middleware/roe.py
      The Rules of Engagement structure and the way an RoE frames authorized
      actions.

  tools/references/killchain.yaml
      The MITRE ATT&CK kill-chain reference used to map objectives to techniques
      and to render the coverage view.

Adaptation notes:
  * The data model and state machine were reconstructed from the above and
    reshaped onto HackPit's persistence: a shared, upsert-only SQLite store keyed
    by engagement session_id, rather than Decepticon's versioned JSON files.
  * The RoE is treated as a written frame the human approves against — advisory,
    NOT a machine veto. HackPit's standing model keeps per-command human approval
    as the actual bound; governance executes nothing and adds no gate.
  * Generation is propose-only: the drafter (backend/governance_draft.py) drafts
    the four documents from the scope + target for a human to edit and approve; it
    executes nothing an engagement acts on.
  * The session engine adopts Decepticon's session MECHANICS but NOT its autonomy:
    Decepticon's agent can send `is_input` to drive an interactive prompt itself,
    which HackPit deliberately does not adopt. Every input path (run a command,
    answer a prompt) is HUMAN-ONLY and source-scan locked to the router; the
    orchestrator/agent/executor/proposer have no path to send input. The engine
    lives on the open /terminal side (never the isolated sandbox), adds no gate,
    and its container is hardcoded — the same containment the raw PTY carries.
    Decepticon's tools/bash source was not present in the tree; the semantics were
    reconstructed from the build spec's description and reproduced faithfully.

